  "text": "To find the value of \\( b \\) such that the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\) has at least two distinct integer roots, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the roots of the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\).\n2. Use these roots as coefficients of the second polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\).\n\nGiven the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\), let's assume the roots are \\( r_1, r_2, \\) and \\( r_3 \\). By Vieta's formulas, we know:\n\n\\[\nr_1 + r_2 + r_3 = -3\n\\]\n\n\\[\nr_1r_2 + r_2r_3 + r_3r_1 = a\n\\]\n\n\\[\nr_1r_2r_3 = -7\n\\]\n\nSince \\( -7 \\) can be factored into three roots, and we are looking for distinct integer roots, the possible sets of factors for \\( -7 \\) are \\( (-1, 1, -7) \\). Given that there are only three roots and they must sum up to \\(-3\\), the roots can only be \\( 1, -7 \\), and another integer. The only integer that satisfies this condition and the sum condition is \\( -1 \\).\n\nSo, the roots of the polynomial are \\( 1, -7, \\) and \\( -1 \\).\n\nNow, we substitute \\( y = x \\) into the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\):\n\n\\[\n1^3 + 3(1^2) + a(1) + 7 = 0 \\implies 1 + 3 + a + 7 = 0 \\implies a + 11 = 0 \\implies a = -11\n\\]\n\nSo, the polynomial becomes \\( y^3 + 3y^2 - 11y + b = 0 \\).\n\nTo find \\( b \\), we subdivide the polynomial:\n\n\\[\n1^3 + 3(1^2) - 11(1) + b = 0 \\implies 1 + 3 - 11 + b = 0 \\implies b = 7\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(-7)^3 + 3(-7)^2 - 11(-7) + b = 0 \\implies -343 + 147 + 77 + b = 0 \\implies b = 123\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(-1)^3 + 3(-1)^2 - 11(-1) + b = 0 \\implies -1 + 3 + 11 + b = 0 \\implies b = -13\n\\]\n\nSo the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 - 11y + b = 0 \\) has roots \\( 1, -7, \\) and \\( -1 \\) if \\( b = -13, 7, \\) or \\( 123 \\). Since we need at least two distinct integer roots, the only valid solution is \\( b = 7 \\).\n\nThus, the value of \\( b \\) is \\(\\boxed{75}\\).",
